Structure in [38;5;10mtranslation/src/main.rs[0m mirrors the C one-for-one ([38;5;10mprint_line[0m, [38;5;10mprint_int_line[0m, [38;5;10mbad[0m, [38;5;10mgood_g2b[0m, [38;5;10mgood_b2g[0m, [38;5;10mgood[0m, [38;5;10mmain[0m), so the divide-by-zero defect is preserved rather than fixed. Three pieces of C semantics needed explicit emulation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mfgets[0m — reads at most 19 bytes and stops [3mafter[23m a newline, returning [38;5;10mNone[0m only when zero bytes were read. Implemented as byte-at-a-time reads over a single shared [38;5;10mStdinLock[0m, so [38;5;10mgood_b2g[0m's read and [38;5;10mbad[0m's read consume the input in the same order and never overrun the line boundary.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10matof[0m — a [38;5;10mstrtod[0m front-end that skips C whitespace, accepts a sign, [38;5;10minf[0m/[38;5;10minfinity[0m/[38;5;10mnan[0m, hex floats, and decimal-with-exponent, truncates at the first NUL, and yields [38;5;10m0.0[0m when nothing converts. The accepted decimal prefix is handed to Rust's correctly-rounded [38;5;10mf64[0m parser so rounding matches glibc; hex floats go through a manual round-to-nearest-even path.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m(int)(100.0 / data)[0m — casting infinity or NaN to [38;5;10mint[0m is undefined in C, and on x86-64 [38;5;10mcvttsd2si[0m produces [38;5;10m0x80000000[0m. Rust's [38;5;10mas i32[0m saturates instead, so [38;5;10mc_double_to_int[0m reproduces the hardware result, which is what makes the divide-by-zero case print [38;5;10m-2147483648[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Note that this hardware-indefinite value is the one place where output depends on the original program's undefined behavior; it matches the gcc/x86-64 build used for comparison, and would differ on a target whose float-to-int conversion saturates or on a build that constant-folds the division.[0m[0m
